 an endogenous variable, as it is influenced by the returns of the stock and other factors within the system.\n\nThere are other models that can be used to forecast returns taking into account the volumes of an asset, such as factor models. Factor models aim to explain the returns of an asset by decomposing them into a set of common factors that capture the underlying economic and financial drivers of the asset's returns. These factors can include macroeconomic variables, industry-specific factors, and other relevant variables that influence the asset's returns.\n\nOne popular factor model is the Fama-French three-factor model, which includes the market risk premium, the size factor, and the value factor to explain the returns of an asset. Other factor models may include additional factors such as momentum, liquidity, or volatility to capture different aspects of an asset's returns.\n\nIn summary, volume is usually regarded as being a non-stationary variable and an endogenous variable in a VAR model. Other models such as factor models can be used to forecast returns taking into account the volumes of an asset by incorporating relevant factors that influence the asset's returns."}
